tests/sys/netpfil/integration: 5BSD PF seam tests
==================================================

5BSD runs OpenBSD PF on the FreeBSD network stack, under 5BSD's capability
plane and jails (docs/book/src/operations/network-provenance.md).  The
FreeBSD suites (tests/sys/net*, tests/sys/netinet*) test the stack and
tests/sys/netpfil/pf plus pf/openbsd test PF itself.  The programs here test
the seams between them:

  vnet_dev      /dev/pf per VNET, devfs rulesets 4 and 5, descriptor binding
  host_limits   pool limits owned by the host vnet
  modules       preloaded modules, pf.ko cloners, kldload vs. the broker, rc.d
  cloners       pflog/pfsync/pflow cloned interfaces in VNET jails
  jail_rule     the "jail" rule option (5BSD extension)
  rdomain       routing domains in jails, rtable/rdomain rules, SO_RTABLE
  sockets       SO_BINDANY, SO_RXQ_OVFL, IP_RECVDSTPORT, divert(4) sockets
  securelevel   pf locked at the prison's securelevel 2 (OpenBSD semantics)
  consumers     periodic, blocklistd-helper, bsnmpd, netstat, tcpdump, pflogd
  dtrace        dtrace_pf(4) probes and the installed pf DTrace scripts
  bridge        pf on if_bridge members, if_enc, prio
  containers    podman's CNI networking: <cni-nat> masquerade, cni-rdr anchors
  names         interfaces sharing a name, interfaces named after groups

The programs read host-wide state and create their own VNET jails, so they
run exclusively in the host vnet (see Makefile).  Run them in a test VM, not
on a working host:

	kyua test -k /usr/tests/sys/netpfil/integration/Kyuafile

Test images must preload the modules the capability plane does not allow
to be loaded after boot (loader.conf): pf_load, dtraceall_load,
if_epair_load, if_bridge_load (see tests/README).
